insert into public.aquasense_knowledge_chunks (title, content, source_title, source_url) values
('Routine aquarium water testing', 'Test aquarium water routinely and before adding fish. At minimum, consider the parameters relevant to the system, including temperature, pH, and chlorine for source water; during a new tank cycle, test ammonia, nitrite, and nitrate as well. A home test kit helps track water changes and filter maintenance. AquaSense temperature and pH sensors do not measure every water-quality parameter, so use appropriate independent tests when needed.', 'Merck Veterinary Manual: Providing a Home for Fish', 'https://www.merckvetmanual.com/all-other-pets/fish/providing-a-home-for-fish'),
('Nitrite and new tank syndrome', 'Nitrite is produced when biofilter bacteria process ammonia and can be toxic to fish at elevated concentrations. During a new aquarium cycle, monitor ammonia, nitrite, and nitrate; do not add fish based only on elapsed time or clear-looking water. If fish are ill or lethargic, test the water promptly because ammonia or nitrite problems can resemble disease. Correct the underlying water-quality problem and maintain adequate biofiltration.', 'Merck Veterinary Manual: Management of Aquarium Fish', 'https://www.merckvetmanual.com/exotic-and-laboratory-animals/aquarium-fish/management-of-aquarium-fish'),
('Nitrate accumulation', 'Nitrate is the later product of biological nitrification and is generally less toxic than ammonia and nitrite, but it can accumulate in aquariums. Test it as part of assessing a tank, especially during cycling or when livestock appear unwell. Plants can use nitrate, and water exchange or suitable filtration can help remove it. Do not infer nitrate concentration from temperature, pH, or water clarity.', 'Merck Veterinary Manual: Providing a Home for Fish', 'https://www.merckvetmanual.com/all-other-pets/fish/providing-a-home-for-fish'),
('Adding fish to an aquarium gradually', 'A biofilter and the rest of an aquarium need time to adjust to additional waste. Adding many fish at once can exceed the system’s current biological capacity and allow ammonia or nitrite to rise. Add livestock gradually, monitor water quality after additions, and consider the species’ adult size, behavior, territory, oxygen needs, and the actual capacity of the filtration system. There is no single fish-per-gallon rule that fits every aquarium.', 'Merck Veterinary Manual: Providing a Home for Fish', 'https://www.merckvetmanual.com/all-other-pets/fish/providing-a-home-for-fish'),
('Acclimating newly arrived fish', 'Before releasing a newly arrived fish, gradually equalize the transport water temperature with the aquarium. Avoid pouring transport-bag water into the established aquarium. Observe the new fish closely after introduction, since stress, territorial aggression, and disease exposure can all affect how it settles in. Follow species-appropriate acclimation guidance, especially when water chemistry differs substantially.', 'Merck Veterinary Manual: Providing a Home for Fish', 'https://www.merckvetmanual.com/all-other-pets/fish/providing-a-home-for-fish'),
('Quarantine tank setup and equipment', 'A separate quarantine aquarium can help observe new fish and reduce the chance of transferring some parasites to the established tank. Keep nets and siphon hoses dedicated to quarantine. A simple setup can use a tank, sponge filter, aeration, and a heater when appropriate for the species. Disinfect and dry the quarantine equipment between uses. Quarantine cannot detect or prevent every pathogen, so it reduces some risks but is not a guarantee.', 'Merck Veterinary Manual: Management of Aquarium Fish', 'https://www.merckvetmanual.com/exotic-and-laboratory-animals/aquarium-fish/management-of-aquarium-fish'),
('Chlorine and chloramine in replacement water', 'Chlorine and monochloramine used in municipal water can harm fish and beneficial aquarium bacteria. Treat replacement water with a product labeled to neutralize the disinfectant present before it contacts fish. Check with the local water utility if you do not know which disinfectant is used. Do not assume that letting water stand removes chloramine; use an appropriate treatment method and follow its label.', 'US EPA: Common Health Questions Related to Monochloramine', 'https://www.epa.gov/sites/default/files/2015-09/documents/can-chloraminated-chlorinated-water-be-used-for-dialysis-or-aquarium.pdf'),
('Water hardness and aquarium species', 'General hardness is primarily a measure of dissolved calcium and magnesium. Fish species differ in the water conditions they tolerate, so check species-specific care requirements and measure source and aquarium water rather than relying on a regional map or tap-water description. Hardness is not the same measurement as pH or alkalinity. Avoid making rapid mineral or pH changes without understanding the species and the water chemistry.', 'USGS: Hardness of Water', 'https://www.usgs.gov/water-science-school/science/hardness-water'),
('Alkalinity and pH buffering', 'Alkalinity is the capacity of water to neutralize acid and is often described as buffering capacity. It is related to, but not identical to, pH: pH is the current acid-base condition, while alkalinity describes resistance to acid-driven change. Low or changing alkalinity can make pH less stable. Measure the relevant parameters when investigating repeated pH swings, and avoid trying to correct pH without identifying the underlying water chemistry.', 'USGS: Alkalinity and Acid Neutralizing Capacity', 'https://pubs.usgs.gov/publication/twri09A6.6'),
('Saltwater aquarium salinity', 'Marine fish require saltwater conditions and freshwater fish require freshwater conditions; do not use freshwater care targets for a marine aquarium. Merck describes 30–33 parts per thousand as a usual salinity range for saltwater environments. Use commercial marine salt and measure salinity with a suitable instrument, following species-specific husbandry guidance. Evaporation removes water but leaves most salt behind, so topping off evaporation with saltwater can raise salinity.', 'Merck Veterinary Manual: Providing a Home for Fish', 'https://www.merckvetmanual.com/all-other-pets/fish/providing-a-home-for-fish'),
('Filter capacity and aquarium stocking', 'The safe number of fish depends on more than tank volume. Filtration capacity, water-quality test results, fish size and behavior, available territory, and oxygen conditions all matter. Overcrowding can stress fish and make water quality harder to maintain. Evaluate the adult size and needs of each species and use ammonia and nitrite trends to check whether the biological filter is keeping up with waste.', 'Merck Veterinary Manual: Providing a Home for Fish', 'https://www.merckvetmanual.com/all-other-pets/fish/providing-a-home-for-fish'),
('Copper medication and aquarium invertebrates', 'Copper treatments require careful species- and system-specific handling. Copper can be extremely toxic to invertebrates and plants, can affect nitrifying biofilter bacteria, and requires monitoring with a test kit suited to the copper formulation. Do not add copper medication to a display aquarium containing invertebrates, or guess a dose. Follow a veterinarian’s direction and the product label, and monitor ammonia and nitrite if treatment may affect biofiltration.', 'Merck Veterinary Manual: Management of Aquarium Fish', 'https://www.merckvetmanual.com/exotic-and-laboratory-animals/aquarium-fish/management-of-aquarium-fish'),
('Checking water quality when fish appear sick', 'Poor water quality is a common environmental contributor to fish illness. When fish show abnormal behavior, first check measurable water conditions such as temperature, pH, chlorine exposure, ammonia, and nitrite as appropriate for the situation. AquaSense readings cover only connected sensors and cannot diagnose disease or measure unconnected parameters. If signs persist or worsen, seek advice from an aquatic veterinarian or qualified fish-health professional rather than treating an unconfirmed diagnosis.', 'Merck Veterinary Manual: Providing a Home for Fish', 'https://www.merckvetmanual.com/all-other-pets/fish/providing-a-home-for-fish')
